  "levels": [
    {
      "id": "1.1",
      "title": "Ask Your Neighbours",
      "story": "A crate of unlabelled fruit has turned up at Fenn's stall. \"Plum or apricot?\" Fenn asks. \"Don't ask me. Ask the fruit next door. If the three most like it are apricots, it is probably an apricot too.\"",
      "everyday": {
        "scene": "A new family moves into your street. Without knowing anything else, you might guess how they vote or what they like from their neighbours.",
        "question": "How could a computer guess something about a new item using only the items already known?",
        "answer": "It can look up the most similar known items and let them vote. That is the whole idea of k-nearest neighbours."
      },
      "lessons": [
        {
          "id": "nearest-vote",
          "title": "Let the neighbours vote",
          "text": [
            "k-nearest neighbours has no real training step. It keeps every example. To label a new item it measures how far the item is from each stored example, picks the k closest, and takes a vote.",
            "The table lists eight stored fruit by how far each is from a new fruit with width 6 and height 4. The closest ones decide."
          ],
          "terms": [
            {
              "term": "k",
              "meaning": "How many neighbours get a vote."
            },
            {
              "term": "Distance",
              "meaning": "How far apart two items are, measured on their features."
            }
          ],
          "table": {
