        {
          "id": "choosing-k",
          "title": "Choosing k",
          "text": [
            "With k = 1 the answer comes from one neighbour, which could be a strange one. With a large k the vote becomes smooth but may include far-off items that are not really similar.",
            "With two classes, an odd k avoids tied votes. There is no best k for everything, so k is tried out on data kept back for testing."
          ],
          "predict": {
            "prompt": "In the table, the nearest fruit is a Plum, but the next two are Apricots. What does k = 3 say?",
            "answer": "Apricot, by 2 votes to 1."
          },
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Wikipedia: k-nearest neighbors algorithm",
              "source": "Wikipedia (linked, not copied)",
              "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/K-nearest_neighbors_algorithm"
            },
            {
              "title": "scikit-learn user guide: Nearest Neighbors",
              "source": "scikit-learn user guide (linked, not copied)",
              "url": "https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/neighbors.html"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "distance-and-scale",
          "title": "Distance and scale",
          "text": [
            "The usual distance is Euclidean: take the gap on each feature, square each gap, add them up and take the square root. For (2, 3) and (5, 7), the gaps are 3 and 4, so the distance is 5.",
            "Features on big scales swamp the rest. A fruit's weight in grams would drown its sweetness score from 1 to 10. Squeezing each feature into a range from 0 to 1 first makes each feature count fairly."
          ],
          "table": {
            "head": [
